WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:10.417
[MUSIC]

00:00:10.417 --> 00:00:13.458
Our workplaces are killing us in several
ways.

00:00:13.458 --> 00:00:15.280
Number one, they are working us to death.

00:00:15.280 --> 00:00:16.650
Too many hours.

00:00:16.650 --> 00:00:18.860
Hours have been shown to be related, for
instance,

00:00:18.860 --> 00:00:22.350
to blood pressure in an almost monotonic
relationship.

00:00:22.350 --> 00:00:27.771
Workplaces are killing us because they
stress us and absence of job control,

00:00:27.771 --> 00:00:33.660
work-family conflict economic insecurity
is obviously stressful.

00:00:33.660 --> 00:00:35.981
So the combination of working us to death
and

00:00:35.981 --> 00:00:38.375
stressing us to death is what's killing
us.

00:00:38.375 --> 00:00:43.083
We understand that people's minds effects
their bodies and

00:00:43.083 --> 00:00:47.542
stress is, of course, a mental state and a
mental attitude.

00:00:47.542 --> 00:00:52.662
So in some sense, it's not that surprising
that things that make you stressed,

00:00:52.662 --> 00:00:58.250
nervous, anxious feel out of control would
have enormous physiological impact.

00:00:58.250 --> 00:01:01.379
And that's what the epidemiological
literature has shown really for

00:01:01.379 --> 00:01:02.593
a long time.

00:01:02.593 --> 00:01:06.123
So our contribution and the research that
we did

00:01:06.123 --> 00:01:11.003
was to take a bunch of stuff that was kind
of sitting there and put it together.

00:01:11.003 --> 00:01:15.463
So there were studies of health effects of
various workplace stressors, and

00:01:15.463 --> 00:01:18.903
there were estimates of the pre,
prevalence of various workplace stressors.

00:01:18.903 --> 00:01:23.433
What we did is put this all together in a
big model to estimate the total

00:01:23.433 --> 00:01:27.107
effects on population health and
healthcare costs.

00:01:27.107 --> 00:01:32.250
[MUSIC]

00:01:32.250 --> 00:01:37.731
What we found when we put all these data
together is that our best estimate is

00:01:37.731 --> 00:01:44.256
that there are about 120,000 excess deaths
annually just in the United States and

00:01:44.256 --> 00:01:49.389
close to $200 billion in excess healthcare
costs as a consequence

00:01:49.389 --> 00:01:54.410
of what we call exposures to harmful
workplace conditions.

00:01:54.410 --> 00:02:00.981
And those would be things such as economic
insecurity absence of health insurance,

00:02:00.981 --> 00:02:07.377
shift work, work-family conflict, things
that either effect directly access

00:02:07.377 --> 00:02:12.917
to healthcare like health insurance, or
put stress and strain on people.

00:02:12.917 --> 00:02:18.125
[MUSIC]

00:02:18.125 --> 00:02:21.890
So when you say, what is the worst, it
depends on the outcome.

00:02:21.890 --> 00:02:23.770
It turns out death is cheap.

00:02:23.770 --> 00:02:28.243
Once you die, your healthcare costs stop,
so the biggest contributors to

00:02:28.243 --> 00:02:31.793
healthcare costs are what I would call
chronic stressors,

00:02:31.793 --> 00:02:35.840
things like work-family conflict and
economic insecurity.

00:02:37.300 --> 00:02:41.530
The single biggest factor affecting
mortality is

00:02:41.530 --> 00:02:46.340
the absence of access to healthcare which
comes from not having health insurance.

00:02:47.440 --> 00:02:53.060
So the causes of costs are a little bit
different than the costs of mortality.

00:02:53.060 --> 00:02:56.830
Because mortality and costs are not
perfectly correlated.

00:02:56.830 --> 00:03:00.520
Things like economic insecurity and
work-family conflict

00:03:00.520 --> 00:03:04.390
had pretty large effects, not as big as
the absence of health insurance or

00:03:04.390 --> 00:03:06.850
work hours, but almost as big.

00:03:06.850 --> 00:03:09.792
So psychology matters as well.

00:03:09.792 --> 00:03:14.583
[MUSIC]

00:03:14.583 --> 00:03:19.984
Well, I think for people employees, they
need to understand that going

00:03:19.984 --> 00:03:25.055
to places where they experience
work-family conflicts, shift work,

00:03:25.055 --> 00:03:30.790
long work hours, economic insecurity,
don't get access to health insurance,

00:03:30.790 --> 00:03:36.290
that this is more than just, you know,
something that's inconvenient.

00:03:36.290 --> 00:03:42.264
This is something that actually affects
their well-being in a deep and profound

00:03:42.264 --> 00:03:47.938
way, and they ought to take these factors
into account as they select their jobs.

00:03:47.938 --> 00:03:50.771
For employers who are concerned about
healthcare costs,

00:03:50.771 --> 00:03:53.333
they should worry a lot about the work
environment.

00:03:53.333 --> 00:03:57.700
At the moment, employers worry mostly
about individual decisions, eating,

00:03:57.700 --> 00:04:02.133
exercise, smoking, drinking, things like
that, or they worry about broad,

00:04:02.133 --> 00:04:06.333
social policy issues, like how we pay for
healthcare in the United States.

00:04:06.333 --> 00:04:10.966
A lot of their excess healthcare costs
come from what happens to people

00:04:10.966 --> 00:04:15.912
every day in the work environment, and
almost everything that, is around

00:04:15.912 --> 00:04:20.958
the work environment are things that
employers could fix if they wanted to.

00:04:20.958 --> 00:04:26.083
[MUSIC]

00:04:26.083 --> 00:04:30.445
Just as we have regulated secondhand smoke
exposure,

00:04:30.445 --> 00:04:34.284
presumably public policy could do
something to,

00:04:34.284 --> 00:04:38.900
intervene and not so much to regulate the
workplace.

00:04:38.900 --> 00:04:40.490
But if you think about it,

00:04:40.490 --> 00:04:44.048
what we're talking about is a form of
social pollution.

00:04:44.048 --> 00:04:49.000
So, 50, 60 ,70 years ago, if I foul the
water or

00:04:49.000 --> 00:04:53.060
foul the air, I did not have to pay for
the cost of that.

00:04:53.060 --> 00:04:58.690
And so, if you had companies incur the
cost

00:04:58.690 --> 00:05:03.630
of laying people off and making people
feel economically insecure,

00:05:03.630 --> 00:05:10.310
if you had companies facing the costs of
not providing people health

00:05:10.310 --> 00:05:15.720
insurance not providing people flexibility
to take care of their family obligations,

00:05:17.330 --> 00:05:22.120
not providing justice and fairness, which
is another one of the workplace exposures,

00:05:22.120 --> 00:05:25.690
then those costs would become internalized
to the employer, and

00:05:25.690 --> 00:05:29.150
they would make better and more
economically rational decisions.

00:05:29.150 --> 00:05:33.120
But at the moment, they just pass the cost
off to the larger society, so

00:05:33.120 --> 00:05:37.150
therefore, they are not very concerned
about this.

00:05:37.150 --> 00:05:39.780
Because at the end of the day if you think
about it

00:05:41.430 --> 00:05:46.280
if an employee gets really sick or really
dies the, they drop out of the workforce,

00:05:46.280 --> 00:05:49.280
and then they're no longer the employer's
responsibility at all.

00:05:49.280 --> 00:05:53.977
So what we need to do with respect to
social pollution or human pollution,

00:05:53.977 --> 00:05:58.221
it's what we have done in the past with
environmental pollution,

00:05:58.221 --> 00:06:02.235
which is to have the employers see the
cost of their actions, so

00:06:02.235 --> 00:06:05.834
they can make more intelligent and sounder
decisions.

00:06:05.834 --> 00:06:10.849
[MUSIC]

